## Protected from diagnosis

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, **MS counts as a disability from the day you are diagnosed**, even if you have no symptoms. You do not have to tell your employer, but you need to for them to make adjustments.

## At work

Reasonable adjustments could include:

- flexible hours, working from home, or a later start
- rest breaks and a place to rest
- a cool workspace or fan
- a parking space near the entrance, and a desk close to the toilet
- time off for appointments and infusions

**Access to Work** can help pay for equipment, taxis or support.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how MS affects you on most days. If your MS varies, think about the whole year. MS can affect many activities, such as **moving around**, **preparing food**, **washing**, **dressing**, **toilet needs** and **reading**. Fatigue counts if it stops you doing things reliably.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

## Other help

You may be able to get a [Blue Badge](/support/travel/blue-badge/), help from the [Motability Scheme](/support/travel/motability-and-car-tax/), or [help with home adaptations](/support/home-and-equipment/).

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
